/* PaKuWord – English daily pack 2026-11-28: new domain Physics (30) + 30 entries in existing domains (ast, med, law, gen, psy) */
window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- PHYSICS ---------- */
{id:"phy_physics",cat:"phy",t:"Physics",
 s:"The science of matter, energy, motion and the forces that connect them.",
 l:"Physics tries to find the simplest rules behind everything that happens, from a ball rolling down a hill to light crossing the universe. Physicists make careful measurements, describe patterns with mathematics and test their ideas with experiments. Many other sciences build on physics: chemistry uses its ideas about atoms, and engineering uses its laws of motion and electricity. Everyday technology, such as phones, fridges and bicycles, works because of principles that physicists worked out.",
 x:"In physics today we timed a pendulum and found that a longer string swings more slowly.",
 f:"The word comes from the Greek word physis, meaning nature.",
 js:"The science of how things move, push, pull, shine and make noise.",
 jl:"Physics asks big questions about the world. Why do things fall down? How does a magnet stick to the fridge? Why is the sky bright in the day? Physicists answer these questions by doing experiments and measuring very carefully.",
 jx:"We did a physics experiment to see which paper plane flew the farthest."},
{id:"phy_force",cat:"phy",t:"Force",
 s:"A push or a pull that can change how an object moves or change its shape.",
 l:"A force always acts in a particular direction and has a size, so physicists draw it as an arrow. Forces can make a still object start moving, speed it up, slow it down, turn it or squash it. Several forces usually act at once: a book on a table is pulled down by gravity and pushed up by the table, and because the two balance, it stays still. Force is measured in newtons, named after Isaac Newton.",
 x:"The wind put so much force on the umbrella that it turned inside out.",
 f:"A force of about one newton is roughly what you feel holding a small apple in your hand.",
 js:"A push or a pull.",
 jl:"When you kick a ball, you push it with a force and it flies away. When you open a drawer, you pull it with a force. Forces can make things start moving, stop, change direction or even change shape, like when you squeeze modelling clay.",
 jx:"I used a lot of force to open the tight lid of the jam jar."},
{id:"phy_gravity",cat:"phy",t:"Gravity",
 s:"The force by which every object with mass pulls on every other object.",
 l:"Gravity is the reason things fall to the ground, the reason we stay on Earth and the reason the Moon circles our planet. The more mass an object has, the stronger its pull, and the farther away you go, the weaker it becomes. Gravity between everyday objects is far too weak to notice, but a whole planet or star produces a very strong pull. Isaac Newton described gravity with a law in the seventeenth century, and Albert Einstein later explained it as the bending of space and time.",
 x:"Because of gravity, the dropped spoon clattered onto the kitchen floor.",
 f:"On the Moon, gravity is only about one sixth as strong as on Earth, so astronauts could jump much higher than at home.",
 js:"The invisible pull that makes things fall down.",
 jl:"When you jump, you always come back down. That is gravity pulling you towards the Earth. Gravity also keeps the Moon going round the Earth and the Earth going round the Sun. Big, heavy things like planets pull the strongest.",
 jx:"Gravity pulled my ice cream off the cone and onto the pavement."},
{id:"phy_mass_weight",cat:"phy",t:"Mass and weight",
 s:"Mass is the amount of matter in an object; weight is the force with which gravity pulls on that mass.",
 l:"In everyday speech we use the two words as if they meant the same, but physicists keep them apart. Mass is measured in kilograms and stays the same wherever the object is. Weight is a force, so it is measured in newtons and depends on the strength of gravity. An astronaut has the same mass on Earth and on the Moon, but weighs much less on the Moon. Bathroom scales actually measure the force and then show it as kilograms.",
 x:"The rock samples have the same mass on Mars as on Earth, but they weigh less there.",
 f:"Until 2019 the kilogram was defined by a metal cylinder kept near Paris; now it is defined using a fixed number from physics called the Planck constant.",
 js:"Mass is how much stuff something has; weight is how hard gravity pulls it.",
 jl:"Your mass is how much of you there is, and it is the same on Earth or on the Moon. Your weight is how strongly gravity pulls you down. On the Moon gravity is weaker, so you would weigh less, even though you are still exactly the same you.",
 jx:"On the Moon my backpack would feel lighter, but it would still hold the same books."},
{id:"phy_energy",cat:"phy",t:"Energy",
 s:"The ability to do work, such as moving something, heating it or making it light up.",
 l:"Energy comes in many forms: movement energy, stored energy in a stretched spring or a raised weight, heat, light, sound, chemical energy in food and fuel, and electrical energy. It is never used up in the sense of disappearing; instead it changes from one form to another. A torch turns the chemical energy of a battery into light and a little heat. Energy is measured in joules, named after the English physicist James Prescott Joule.",
 x:"A wind turbine turns the energy of moving air into electrical energy.",
 f:"James Prescott Joule, who gave his name to the unit of energy, also helped run his family's brewery near Manchester.",
 js:"What makes things move, warm up, light up or make a sound.",
 jl:"You need energy to run, jump and think, and you get it from food. A car gets energy from fuel or a battery. A lamp turns electrical energy into light. Energy keeps changing from one kind into another, a bit like water being poured from one cup into another.",
 jx:"After breakfast I had loads of energy for the school trip."},
{id:"phy_conservation_energy",cat:"phy",t:"Conservation of energy",
 s:"The law that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form into another.",
 l:"If you add up all the energy in a closed system before and after something happens, the total is the same. When a ball falls, its stored energy of height turns into movement energy; when it hits the ground, that becomes sound and a little heat. Machines never give out more energy than they take in, and some is always turned into heat that is hard to use. That is why a perpetual motion machine, one that runs forever without fuel, is impossible.",
 x:"Because of the conservation of energy, the swing can never rise higher than the point you let go from without a push.",
 f:"In 1918 the mathematician Emmy Noether showed that the conservation of energy follows from the fact that the laws of physics do not change over time.",
 js:"Energy never vanishes; it just changes into another kind.",
 jl:"Energy is like a magician that changes costumes but never disappears. When you ride a sledge down a hill, the energy of being high up turns into speed. When you stop, it turns into a little warmth and the swish of snow. The total amount stays the same.",
 jx:"The teacher said the energy from my sledge ride did not disappear; it turned into heat and noise."},
{id:"phy_friction",cat:"phy",t:"Friction",
 s:"A force that resists the sliding or rolling of one surface over another.",
 l:"Friction appears whenever surfaces touch and try to move past each other, because even smooth-looking surfaces are bumpy at a tiny scale. It slows moving objects and turns some of their movement energy into heat, which is why rubbing your hands warms them. Friction is often useful: without it shoes would slip, car tyres could not grip the road and a knot would undo itself. Engineers reduce unwanted friction with oil, ball bearings or smooth materials.",
 x:"The new tyres have more friction, so the bike does not skid on wet roads.",
 f:"Leonardo da Vinci studied friction in his notebooks and noticed that it does not depend on how large the touching area is, a rule rediscovered by Guillaume Amontons in 1699.",
 js:"The force that makes things rub and slow down.",
 jl:"Try sliding a toy car on a carpet and then on a smooth floor. On the carpet it stops quickly, because there is more friction. Friction is what lets your shoes grip the ground, so you do not slip. When you rub your hands together, friction makes them warm.",
 jx:"My socks slid on the shiny floor because there was hardly any friction."},
{id:"phy_inertia",cat:"phy",t:"Inertia",
 s:"The tendency of an object to keep doing what it is doing: to stay still, or to keep moving at the same speed in a straight line.",
 l:"An object does not need a force to keep moving; it needs a force to change its motion. On Earth, moving things slow down because friction and air resistance act on them. Inertia explains why passengers lurch forward when a bus brakes suddenly: their bodies keep moving after the bus slows. The more mass an object has, the greater its inertia, so a loaded lorry is much harder to start or stop than a bicycle. Seat belts exist because of inertia.",
 x:"When the tram braked sharply, inertia pushed all the standing passengers forward.",
 f:"The word comes from the Latin iners, meaning idle or inactive.",
 js:"Things like to keep doing what they are already doing.",
 jl:"If a ball is lying still, it stays still until someone kicks it. If it is rolling, it wants to keep rolling. That is called inertia. When a car stops suddenly, your body wants to keep going forward. That is why we always wear seat belts.",
 jx:"When the bus stopped, inertia made my water bottle roll to the front."},
{id:"phy_velocity",cat:"phy",t:"Speed and velocity",
 s:"Speed is how fast something moves; velocity is speed in a particular direction.",
 l:"Speed tells you how much distance is covered in a certain time, for example fifty kilometres per hour. Velocity adds the direction, such as fifty kilometres per hour heading north. The difference matters: a car driving round a roundabout at a steady speed is constantly changing its velocity, because its direction keeps changing. Physicists usually measure speed in metres per second. Average speed is the total distance divided by the total time.",
 x:"The train's speed stayed the same, but its velocity changed when the track curved east.",
 f:"Light in empty space travels at nearly three hundred thousand kilometres per second, while sound in air covers only about three hundred and forty metres in a second.",
 js:"How fast something goes, and in which direction.",
 jl:"Speed tells you how fast you are moving, like a slow snail or a fast cheetah. Velocity also says where you are going, such as fast towards the school gate. If you run round and round in a circle at the same speed, your direction keeps changing.",
 jx:"I measured my running speed on the school field with a stopwatch."},
{id:"phy_acceleration",cat:"phy",t:"Acceleration",
 s:"The rate at which velocity changes: speeding up, slowing down or changing direction.",
 l:"When a car goes from standing still to fifty kilometres per hour in a few seconds, it accelerates. Slowing down is also acceleration, sometimes called deceleration, and so is turning a corner. Acceleration is caused by a force, and the same force gives a light object more acceleration than a heavy one. Near the Earth's surface, falling objects accelerate by about nine point eight metres per second every second, if air resistance is small.",
 x:"The rollercoaster's acceleration pressed us back into our seats.",
 f:"In 1971 the Apollo 15 astronaut David Scott dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon, where there is no air, and they hit the ground at the same moment.",
 js:"Getting faster, getting slower or turning.",
 jl:"When you start pedalling your bike and go faster and faster, you are accelerating. When you squeeze the brakes, you slow down, and that is a kind of acceleration too. Even turning a corner counts, because your direction changes.",
 jx:"The race car's acceleration was so quick that it was gone in a flash."},
{id:"phy_newton_laws",cat:"phy",t:"Newton's laws of motion",
 s:"Three basic rules, described by Isaac Newton, that explain how forces affect the movement of objects.",
 l:"The first law says that an object stays still or keeps moving in a straight line at a steady speed unless a force acts on it. The second says that a force makes an object accelerate, and the heavier the object, the more force is needed for the same acceleration. The third says that forces come in pairs: when you push on something, it pushes back on you equally hard in the opposite direction. These laws are still used to design bridges, cars and space missions.",
 x:"Rockets work because of Newton's third law: hot gas is pushed down, so the rocket is pushed up.",
 f:"Newton published the laws in 1687 in a book written in Latin, Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica, usually called simply the Principia.",
 js:"Three rules about how pushes and pulls make things move.",
 jl:"Isaac Newton noticed three rules. Things keep still or keep moving unless something pushes or pulls them. A bigger push makes things speed up more. And when you push something, it pushes you back, like when you push off from the side of a swimming pool.",
 jx:"When I pushed my friend on the skates, we both rolled backwards, just like Newton's third law says."},
{id:"phy_atom",cat:"phy",t:"Atom",
 s:"The tiny basic unit of a chemical element, made of a nucleus with electrons around it.",
 l:"Everything around us is built from atoms. Each atom has a very small, heavy centre called the nucleus, made of protons and neutrons, surrounded by much lighter electrons. The number of protons decides which element the atom is: one for hydrogen, six for carbon, eight for oxygen. Atoms are so small that millions of them would fit across the full stop at the end of this sentence. Most of an atom is empty space.",
 x:"A water molecule is made of two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom.",
 f:"The name comes from the Greek atomos, meaning uncuttable, because ancient thinkers imagined atoms as the smallest pieces that could not be divided; we now know they can be split.",
 js:"A tiny building block that everything is made of.",
 jl:"Everything around you, your desk, the air and even you, is made of tiny pieces called atoms. They are far too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope. There are different kinds of atoms, like different colours of building bricks, and they join together to make all the things in the world.",
 jx:"Our teacher said the pencil in my hand is made of billions and billions of atoms."},
{id:"phy_electron",cat:"phy",t:"Electron",
 s:"A tiny particle with a negative electric charge that moves around the nucleus of an atom.",
 l:"Electrons are much lighter than the protons and neutrons in the nucleus. They are held near the nucleus by electric attraction, because opposite charges attract. Electrons can move from one atom to another, and their movement through a wire is what we call an electric current. The way electrons are arranged in an atom decides how it reacts chemically. The electron was identified in 1897 by the physicist J. J. Thomson.",
 x:"When you rub a balloon on your jumper, electrons move onto the balloon and it sticks to the wall.",
 f:"The name comes from the Greek word for amber, because people in ancient times noticed that rubbed amber attracts light things like feathers.",
 js:"A tiny bit of an atom that carries electricity.",
 jl:"Inside every atom there are tiny particles called electrons that zoom around the middle. They are so small that nobody can see them. When lots of electrons flow along a wire, we get electricity that can light a lamp.",
 jx:"Rubbing the balloon on my hair moved electrons and made my hair stand up."},
{id:"phy_electric_current",cat:"phy",t:"Electric current",
 s:"The flow of electric charge, usually electrons, through a material such as a wire.",
 l:"A current flows only when there is a complete loop, called a circuit, and something that pushes the charge around, such as a battery. Materials that let current pass easily, like copper, are conductors; materials that block it, like plastic and rubber, are insulators. Current is measured in amperes, or amps, named after the French scientist André-Marie Ampère. A switch works by opening or closing the gap in a circuit.",
 x:"The bulb lit up as soon as we closed the circuit and the current could flow.",
 f:"Diagrams show current flowing from plus to minus, but the electrons actually move the other way; the direction was agreed long before the electron was discovered.",
 js:"Electricity flowing along a wire.",
 jl:"Electric current is like a river of electricity moving through a wire. It can only flow if the path makes a full circle, from the battery, through the lamp and back again. Electricity from wall sockets is very dangerous, so we only do experiments with small batteries.",
 jx:"When I put the last wire in place, the current flowed and the buzzer beeped."},
{id:"phy_voltage",cat:"phy",t:"Voltage",
 s:"The electrical push that drives current around a circuit, measured in volts.",
 l:"Voltage, also called potential difference, is a measure of how much energy each bit of electric charge carries between two points. A higher voltage can push a larger current through the same device. A small battery gives about one and a half volts, a car battery about twelve, and household sockets in Europe supply about two hundred and thirty volts, which is enough to be dangerous. The unit is named after the Italian scientist Alessandro Volta.",
 x:"Check the voltage on the charger before plugging in a laptop bought abroad.",
 f:"In 1800 Alessandro Volta built the first electric battery, a stack of zinc and copper discs separated by cloth soaked in salt water.",
 js:"How hard electricity is pushed along a wire.",
 jl:"Voltage is like the pressure that pushes water through a hose. A small battery gives a gentle push, enough for a torch or a toy. Wall sockets give a very strong push, so they can hurt you badly. Never poke anything into a socket.",
 jx:"My remote control needs two small batteries with a low voltage."},
{id:"phy_magnetism",cat:"phy",t:"Magnetism",
 s:"A force produced by magnets and by moving electric charges that can attract or repel certain materials.",
 l:"Every magnet has two ends, a north pole and a south pole. Opposite poles attract and like poles push each other away. Magnets pull strongly on iron, nickel and cobalt but hardly at all on paper, wood or most other metals. Electricity and magnetism are closely linked: a current in a wire creates a magnetic field, which is how electric motors and loudspeakers work. The Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet, which is why a compass needle points roughly north.",
 x:"We used magnetism to sort the steel paper clips from the plastic beads.",
 f:"The Earth's magnetic field has flipped direction many times in the planet's history; the last full reversal happened roughly seven hundred and eighty thousand years ago.",
 js:"The invisible force that makes magnets stick or push apart.",
 jl:"A magnet can pull some metal things towards it, like paper clips or the fridge door. If you hold two magnets the right way, they snap together. Turn one round and they push each other away. A compass works because the Earth is like a huge magnet.",
 jx:"I used a magnet to pick up all the pins that fell on the floor."},
{id:"phy_wave",cat:"phy",t:"Wave",
 s:"A disturbance that carries energy from place to place without carrying the material along with it.",
 l:"When you drop a stone into a pond, ripples spread outwards, but the water itself mostly moves up and down in place. Sound, light, radio signals and earthquake tremors all travel as waves. Waves are described by their wavelength, the distance from one peak to the next, and their frequency, the number of peaks passing each second, measured in hertz. Waves can bounce off surfaces, bend and spread around corners.",
 x:"The waves from the passing boat rocked our little canoe.",
 f:"A stadium wave, when fans stand up and sit down in turn, shows the idea well: the wave travels around the stadium while every person stays in their seat.",
 js:"A wobble that travels and carries energy.",
 jl:"Throw a pebble into a puddle and watch the rings spread out. Those are waves. The water does not rush away; it just bobs up and down while the wave travels. Sound and light travel as waves too, even though we cannot see them wiggle.",
 jx:"We made a wave with a long skipping rope by flicking one end."},
{id:"phy_sound",cat:"phy",t:"Sound",
 s:"Vibrations that travel through air, water or solids as waves and can be heard.",
 l:"Sound is made when something vibrates, such as a guitar string, our vocal cords or a loudspeaker. The vibrations push on the particles of the air, which push on their neighbours, and the wave travels to our ears. Sound needs a material to travel through, so there is no sound in the near-empty space between planets. A faster vibration gives a higher pitch, and bigger vibrations sound louder. Loudness is often measured in decibels.",
 x:"Count the seconds between the lightning and the thunder: about three seconds means the storm is one kilometre away.",
 f:"In 1947 the American pilot Chuck Yeager became the first person confirmed to fly faster than the speed of sound, in a rocket plane called the Bell X-1.",
 js:"What we hear when something shakes and makes the air wobble.",
 jl:"When you pluck a rubber band, it shakes very fast. The shaking makes the air wobble, and the wobbles travel to your ears. That is sound. Fast wobbles make high squeaky sounds, and slow wobbles make low rumbly sounds.",
 jx:"I put my hand on my throat and felt the sound when I hummed."},
{id:"phy_refraction",cat:"phy",t:"Refraction",
 s:"The bending of light, or another wave, when it passes from one material into another.",
 l:"Light travels at different speeds in different materials, for example more slowly in water or glass than in air. When it crosses the boundary at an angle, it changes direction. That is why a straw in a glass of water looks broken at the surface and why a swimming pool looks shallower than it really is. Lenses in glasses, cameras and microscopes use refraction to focus light. Rainbows form when sunlight is refracted and reflected inside raindrops.",
 x:"Because of refraction, the coin at the bottom of the pool looked closer than it was.",
 f:"Isaac Newton used a glass prism to show that white light is a mixture of colours, each of which bends by a slightly different amount.",
 js:"Light bending when it goes into water or glass.",
 jl:"Put a pencil in a glass of water and look from the side. The pencil seems to bend at the water line. It is not really bent; the light changes direction as it goes from water into air. Glasses help people see by bending light in a clever way.",
 jx:"My spoon looked snapped in half in the glass of water."},
{id:"phy_spectrum",cat:"phy",t:"Electromagnetic spectrum",
 s:"The whole range of electromagnetic waves, from radio waves to gamma rays, of which visible light is a small part.",
 l:"Light is just one kind of electromagnetic wave. Waves with longer wavelengths than visible light include infrared, microwaves and radio waves; waves with shorter wavelengths include ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays. All of them travel at the speed of light in empty space. We use different parts for different jobs: radio for broadcasting, microwaves for cooking and mobile phones, infrared in remote controls, X-rays for looking inside the body.",
 x:"Astronomers study the sky across the electromagnetic spectrum, not only in visible light.",
 f:"In 1800 the astronomer William Herschel placed thermometers beyond the red end of a spectrum made by a prism and found that they warmed up, which led to the discovery of infrared.",
 js:"The big family of invisible and visible light waves.",
 jl:"The light we see is just one member of a big family of waves. Some family members are invisible to us, like the radio waves that carry music, the infrared from a TV remote, or the ultraviolet from the Sun that can burn your skin.",
 jx:"Our TV remote uses invisible infrared light from the electromagnetic spectrum."},
{id:"phy_temperature",cat:"phy",t:"Temperature and heat",
 s:"Temperature measures how hot or cold something is; heat is energy that flows from a hotter object to a cooler one.",
 l:"The particles that make up a substance are always moving, and temperature tells us how fast they move on average. Heat is the energy that passes between objects because of a difference in temperature, and it always flows from hot to cold. A bathtub of warm water holds much more heat energy than a cup of boiling tea, even though the tea has a higher temperature. Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius or in kelvins.",
 x:"The temperature of the soup dropped quickly as heat flowed into the cold bowl.",
 f:"When Anders Celsius created his scale in 1742, he set zero as the boiling point of water and one hundred as the freezing point; the scale was turned the other way round soon after.",
 js:"How hot or cold something is, and the warmth that moves from hot to cold.",
 jl:"A thermometer shows the temperature, so you know if it is a freezing winter day or a hot summer one. Heat always moves from warm things to cold things. That is why a hot drink cools down and an ice cube in your hand melts.",
 jx:"The nurse checked my temperature with a thermometer."},
{id:"phy_states_matter",cat:"phy",t:"States of matter",
 s:"The different forms that matter can take, mainly solid, liquid and gas.",
 l:"In a solid, particles are packed tightly and vibrate in place, so it keeps its shape. In a liquid, they are still close but can slide past one another, so a liquid flows and takes the shape of its container. In a gas, particles are far apart and move freely, filling any space. Heating or cooling can change one state into another: melting, freezing, boiling, condensing. A fourth state, plasma, is a very hot gas with electrically charged particles.",
 x:"Water is the easiest example of the states of matter: ice, liquid water and steam.",
 f:"Plasma is the most common state of visible matter in the universe, because stars, including the Sun, are made of it.",
 js:"The different ways stuff can be: solid, liquid or gas.",
 jl:"Water can be solid ice, runny liquid or invisible steam. These are called states of matter. A solid keeps its shape, a liquid flows and fills a cup, and a gas spreads out everywhere. Warming or cooling something can change it from one state to another.",
 jx:"My ice lolly changed from solid to liquid in the hot sunshine."},
{id:"phy_density",cat:"phy",t:"Density",
 s:"How much mass is packed into a certain volume of a material.",
 l:"Density is mass divided by volume. A kilogram of feathers and a kilogram of iron have the same mass, but the iron takes up far less space, so it is much denser. Objects that are less dense than water float, and denser ones sink, which is why oil forms a layer on top of water. Ships made of steel float because their hollow shape, full of air, gives the whole ship a lower average density than water.",
 x:"The egg sank in fresh water but floated in very salty water, which has a higher density.",
 f:"Ice is less dense than liquid water, which is unusual; because ice floats, lakes freeze from the top down and fish can survive the winter below.",
 js:"How tightly stuff is packed into a space.",
 jl:"A ball of cotton wool and a pebble can be the same size, but the pebble is much heavier. That is because it is denser: more stuff is packed into the same space. Things that are less dense than water float, like a rubber duck, and denser things sink, like a stone.",
 jx:"Our density test showed that the apple floats but the potato sinks."},
{id:"phy_pressure",cat:"phy",t:"Pressure",
 s:"The force pressing on a surface divided by the area it presses on.",
 l:"The same force can produce very different pressures. A sharp knife cuts well because its force is focused on a tiny edge, while snowshoes spread a person's weight over a large area so they do not sink. Liquids and gases also exert pressure: the air around us presses on everything, and water pressure increases the deeper you dive. Pressure is measured in pascals, named after the French thinker Blaise Pascal; weather forecasts use hectopascals.",
 x:"The air pressure in the bike tyres was too low, so pedalling felt hard.",
 f:"In 1643 the Italian scientist Evangelista Torricelli made the first mercury barometer, an instrument for measuring air pressure.",
 js:"How hard something presses on a surface.",
 jl:"If you press your hand on soft sand, it leaves a shallow print. If you press with one finger, using the same push, the finger sinks deeper. That is because the push is squeezed into a smaller space, so the pressure is bigger.",
 jx:"Dad pumped air into the football until the pressure was just right."},
{id:"phy_radioactivity",cat:"phy",t:"Radioactivity",
 s:"The process in which unstable atomic nuclei break down by themselves and give off radiation.",
 l:"Some atoms have nuclei that are not stable. Over time they change into other atoms and release energy as radiation. Each radioactive substance has a half-life, the time it takes for half of its atoms to decay, which can be a fraction of a second or billions of years. Radiation can damage living cells, so it is handled with care, but it is also very useful: in medicine, in smoke detectors, in dating ancient objects and in nuclear power stations.",
 x:"The archaeologists used radioactivity in carbon to estimate how old the wooden beam was.",
 f:"Radioactivity was discovered by Henri Becquerel in 1896, and the word radioactivity itself was coined by Marie and Pierre Curie.",
 js:"When some atoms slowly break apart and give off invisible rays.",
 jl:"A few kinds of atoms are a bit wobbly inside. Over time they break apart and send out invisible energy called radiation. Too much of it is harmful, so scientists use special protection. But doctors also use it carefully to look inside the body and to treat illnesses.",
 jx:"Grandpa told me the old clock's glowing numbers used to contain something radioactive."},
{id:"phy_relativity",cat:"phy",t:"Theory of relativity",
 s:"Albert Einstein's theories describing how space, time, motion and gravity are connected.",
 l:"Einstein published the special theory of relativity in 1905. It says that the speed of light is the same for everyone and that time passes more slowly for objects moving very fast compared with an observer. It also gave the famous equation E equals m c squared, showing that mass and energy are linked. The general theory of relativity, from 1915, explains gravity as the bending of space and time by mass. Both theories have been confirmed by many experiments.",
 x:"Satellite navigation would drift off by kilometres each day if engineers ignored the theory of relativity.",
 f:"Clocks on GPS satellites tick at a slightly different rate from clocks on the ground, and the system has to correct for this effect predicted by relativity.",
 js:"Einstein's big ideas about space, time and very fast travel.",
 jl:"Albert Einstein discovered that time and space can stretch and squeeze. For something going extremely fast, almost as fast as light, time runs a little slower. We do not notice it in everyday life, but scientists have measured it with very precise clocks.",
 jx:"In the library I found a picture book about Einstein and his theory of relativity."},
{id:"phy_en_roygbiv",cat:"phy",only:1,t:"Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain",
 s:"A traditional British memory sentence for the order of the rainbow colours: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.",
 l:"The first letter of each word matches a colour of the rainbow in order, so pupils across Britain learn the sentence in science lessons. It refers to Richard, Duke of York, who was defeated and killed at the Battle of Wakefield in 1460 during the Wars of the Roses. Some people shorten the colours to the made-up name Roy G. Biv instead. In reality the spectrum changes smoothly, and the division into seven named colours is a human choice.",
 x:"I couldn't remember whether indigo came before violet, so I said Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain in my head.",
 f:"Isaac Newton chose to name seven colours in the spectrum partly because he linked them to the seven notes of a musical scale.",
 js:"A silly sentence that helps you remember the rainbow's colours.",
 jl:"Each word starts with the same letter as a rainbow colour: Richard for red, Of for orange, York for yellow, Gave for green, Battle for blue, In for indigo and Vain for violet. Say the sentence and you will never mix up the colours again.",
 jx:"We drew a rainbow and wrote Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain under it."},
{id:"phy_en_christmas_lectures",cat:"phy",only:1,t:"Royal Institution Christmas Lectures",
 s:"A series of lively science lectures for young people given every winter at the Royal Institution in London.",
 l:"Each year a leading scientist presents a set of talks on one topic, packed with demonstrations, for an audience of children and teenagers. The lectures were started in 1825 and Michael Faraday, who worked at the Royal Institution, gave many of them himself. They are recorded and broadcast on television over the Christmas holidays, so families all over the country can watch. Apart from a pause during the Second World War, they have been held every year. Speakers have covered everything from the chemistry of a candle to computers and the human body.",
 x:"Every Christmas we watch the Royal Institution Christmas Lectures together after lunch.",
 f:"Michael Faraday's Christmas talks about the science of a burning candle were published as a book, The Chemical History of a Candle, which is still read today.",
 js:"Fun science shows for children in London every Christmas.",
 jl:"Every winter a famous scientist gives exciting science talks in London, with explosions, experiments and volunteers from the audience. Children sit in a big round lecture theatre to watch. The talks are also shown on TV at Christmas, so you can watch them at home.",
 jx:"In the Christmas lecture the scientist made a balloon freeze with liquid nitrogen."},
{id:"phy_en_cavendish",cat:"phy",only:1,t:"Cavendish Laboratory",
 s:"The physics department of the University of Cambridge, where many major discoveries were made.",
 l:"The Cavendish Laboratory opened in 1874, and its first director was James Clerk Maxwell, who explained light as an electromagnetic wave. In 1897 J. J. Thomson identified the electron there. Researchers under Ernest Rutherford studied the atomic nucleus, and in 1932 John Cockcroft and Ernest Walton split atomic nuclei with an accelerator there. In 1953 Francis Crick and James Watson worked out the double-helix structure of DNA in the same laboratory, using evidence that included X-ray images by Rosalind Franklin.",
 x:"She hopes to do her physics doctorate at the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge.",
 f:"The laboratory is named after the scientist Henry Cavendish and his family; its building was paid for by his relative William Cavendish, Duke of Devonshire, who was the university's chancellor.",
 js:"A famous science lab in Cambridge where big discoveries happened.",
 jl:"In the city of Cambridge there is a laboratory where scientists have made many exciting discoveries. One scientist there found the tiny electron. Others discovered the shape of DNA, the instructions inside living things, which looks like a twisted ladder.",
 jx:"Our teacher showed us a photo of the Cavendish Laboratory where the electron was found."},
{id:"phy_en_kelvin",cat:"phy",only:1,t:"Lord Kelvin",
 s:"William Thomson, a nineteenth-century physicist from Glasgow, after whom the kelvin unit of temperature is named.",
 l:"William Thomson was born in Belfast in 1824 and became a professor at the University of Glasgow when he was only twenty-two. He worked out that there is a lowest possible temperature, absolute zero, and the scale that starts there is called the Kelvin scale. Zero kelvin equals minus two hundred and seventy-three point one five degrees Celsius. He also helped make the first transatlantic telegraph cable work and invented improved ship's compasses. In 1892 he was made Baron Kelvin.",
 x:"Scientists give temperatures in kelvins, the unit named after Lord Kelvin.",
 f:"He took the title Kelvin from the River Kelvin, which flows past the University of Glasgow.",
 js:"A scientist from Glasgow who found the coldest possible temperature.",
 jl:"Lord Kelvin was a clever scientist who worked in Glasgow long ago. He figured out that things cannot get colder than a certain point, called absolute zero. Scientists still use a temperature scale named after him.",
 jx:"We learned that Lord Kelvin's scale begins at the coldest temperature there can be."},
/* ---------- ASTRONOMY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"ast_star",cat:"ast",t:"Star",
 s:"A huge ball of hot gas that produces light and heat by nuclear fusion in its core.",
 l:"In the centre of a star, hydrogen nuclei are squeezed together so hard that they fuse into helium, releasing enormous amounts of energy. That energy slowly makes its way out and shines into space as light. Stars differ in size, brightness and colour: the hottest look blue-white and cooler ones look orange or red. They are born in clouds of gas and dust and can shine for millions or billions of years. The Sun is an ordinary, medium-sized star.",
 x:"Away from the city lights, we could see thousands of stars.",
 f:"The nearest star after the Sun is Proxima Centauri, about four point two light-years away.",
 js:"A giant, glowing ball of hot gas far away in space.",
 jl:"Stars look like tiny twinkling dots, but they are enormous balls of burning-hot gas, many of them much bigger than the Sun. They look small only because they are so far away. Our Sun is a star too; it just happens to be very close to us.",
 jx:"I made a wish on the first star I saw in the evening sky."},
{id:"ast_sun",cat:"ast",t:"Sun",
 s:"The star at the centre of our Solar System, which gives Earth light and warmth.",
 l:"The Sun is a ball of hot plasma, mostly hydrogen and helium, about one hundred and nine times as wide as Earth. It formed about four and a half billion years ago and is roughly halfway through its life. Its energy drives Earth's weather and climate and allows plants to grow. Sunlight takes a little over eight minutes to reach us. Never look directly at the Sun, even through sunglasses, because it can permanently damage your eyes.",
 x:"The Sun rose over the hills just after seven o'clock.",
 f:"The Sun contains about ninety-nine point eight percent of all the mass in the Solar System.",
 js:"Our star, which gives us daylight and warmth.",
 jl:"The Sun is a star, and it is the closest one to us. It is so big that more than a million Earths could fit inside it. It gives us light and warmth, and plants need it to grow. Never look straight at the Sun, because it can hurt your eyes.",
 jx:"We put on sun cream before playing outside in the Sun."},
{id:"ast_tides",cat:"ast",t:"Tides",
 s:"The regular rise and fall of the sea level, caused mainly by the Moon's gravity.",
 l:"The Moon pulls on the whole Earth, but slightly more strongly on the side facing it and slightly less on the far side. This stretches the oceans into two bulges, so most coasts have two high tides and two low tides roughly every day. The Sun adds its own smaller pull: when Sun and Moon line up at new and full moon, tides are larger, called spring tides; at quarter moons they are smaller, called neap tides. Enclosed seas have very small tides.",
 x:"We waited for low tide so we could explore the rock pools.",
 f:"The Bay of Fundy in Canada has some of the largest tides in the world, with the water level changing by up to about sixteen metres.",
 js:"The sea coming in and going out again every day.",
 jl:"At the seaside, the water slowly creeps up the beach and later slides back out again. These are tides. They happen because the Moon pulls on the oceans with its gravity. At low tide you can find crabs and shells in pools between the rocks.",
 jx:"Our sandcastle was washed away when the tide came in."},
{id:"ast_satellite",cat:"ast",t:"Satellite",
 s:"An object that orbits a planet or another larger body, either natural, like the Moon, or built by people.",
 l:"The Moon is Earth's natural satellite, and other planets have many moons of their own. Artificial satellites are machines launched into orbit by rockets. They relay television and phone signals, help us navigate, photograph the weather, watch forests and ice sheets, and study the universe. A satellite stays up because it moves sideways so fast that, as it falls towards Earth, the surface curves away beneath it. Thousands of working satellites now circle our planet.",
 x:"The weather forecast uses pictures of the clouds taken by a satellite.",
 f:"The first artificial satellite, Sputnik 1, was launched by the Soviet Union on 4 October 1957 and sent out simple beeping radio signals.",
 js:"Something that goes round and round a planet.",
 jl:"The Moon goes round the Earth, so it is a satellite. People also build satellites and send them into space on rockets. They help us watch TV, find our way with maps on phones and see what the weather will be like tomorrow.",
 jx:"Dad said the moving dot of light in the sky was a satellite."},
{id:"ast_solar_system",cat:"ast",t:"Solar System",
 s:"The Sun and everything that orbits it: planets, moons, dwarf planets, asteroids and comets.",
 l:"Our Solar System formed about four and a half billion years ago from a spinning cloud of gas and dust. In the middle is the Sun, which holds everything together with its gravity. Around it move eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets such as Pluto, the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, and icy bodies far beyond Neptune. Its outer edge is hard to define, because the Sun's gravity reaches very far. It is one of many planetary systems in the Milky Way.",
 x:"We built a model of the Solar System with balls of different sizes.",
 f:"The space probe Voyager 1, launched in 1977, became the first human-made object to leave the bubble of the Sun's wind and enter interstellar space, in 2012.",
 js:"The Sun and all the planets and other things that go round it.",
 jl:"Our Solar System is like a big family with the Sun in the middle. Eight planets go round it, including Earth. Many planets have moons, and there are also rocky asteroids and icy comets. Earth is the only place in it where we know there is life.",
 jx:"My bedroom ceiling has glow-in-the-dark stickers of the Solar System."},
{id:"ast_en_herschel",cat:"ast",only:1,t:"William Herschel",
 s:"A German-born musician and astronomer who discovered the planet Uranus from his garden in Bath in 1781.",
 l:"William Herschel moved to England as a young man and worked as a musician, becoming an organist in Bath. Fascinated by astronomy, he built his own powerful telescopes, grinding the mirrors by hand. On 13 March 1781 he spotted an object that turned out to be a new planet, the first discovered with a telescope. He wanted to name it after King George III, who then appointed him as an astronomer. His sister Caroline Herschel worked alongside him and discovered several comets herself.",
 x:"In Bath you can visit the house where William Herschel discovered Uranus.",
 f:"Caroline Herschel received a salary from King George III for her work, which made her one of the first women to be paid for scientific work.",
 js:"A music teacher who found a new planet with his home-made telescope.",
 jl:"William Herschel loved music and stars. He built his own telescopes at home in the city of Bath. One night he spotted something new in the sky. It was a planet nobody had seen before: Uranus. His sister Caroline helped him and found comets herself.",
 jx:"We read that William Herschel's sister Caroline was a brilliant comet hunter."},
/* ---------- MEDICINE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"m_immune_system",cat:"med",t:"Immune system",
 s:"The body's network of cells, tissues and organs that defends it against germs and other threats.",
 l:"The immune system includes white blood cells, antibodies, the lymph nodes, the spleen, the bone marrow and the thymus. Its first line of defence reacts quickly to anything unfamiliar; a second, slower part learns to recognise particular germs and remembers them. That memory is why many illnesses are milder or do not return, and it is what vaccines train. Sometimes the immune system overreacts, as in allergies, or attacks the body's own tissues, as in autoimmune diseases.",
 x:"After the cold, her immune system remembered that virus.",
 f:"T cells, a key type of white blood cell, are named after the thymus, a small organ in the chest where they mature.",
 js:"Your body's own team of defenders against germs.",
 jl:"Inside you there is a clever defence team called the immune system. When germs get in, special cells find them and fight them off. Afterwards the team remembers those germs, so next time it can beat them much faster. Sleep and healthy food help it work well.",
 jx:"I had a sniffle, but my immune system fought it off in a few days."},
{id:"m_stethoscope",cat:"med",t:"Stethoscope",
 s:"A medical instrument for listening to sounds inside the body, such as the heart and lungs.",
 l:"A stethoscope has a flat chest piece that picks up sounds, tubes that carry them and earpieces for the doctor. By listening, a doctor or nurse can hear the rhythm of the heart, the flow of air in the lungs and the gurgling of the gut. Unusual sounds, such as a heart murmur or crackles in the lungs, can be clues that further tests are needed. It is also used with a cuff to measure blood pressure by hand.",
 x:"The doctor warmed the stethoscope in her hand before listening to my chest.",
 f:"The French doctor René Laennec invented it in 1816, starting with a rolled-up tube of paper; the name comes from the Greek words for chest and to look at.",
 js:"The doctor's tool for listening to your heart and breathing.",
 jl:"A stethoscope has two earpieces, a long tube and a round end. The doctor puts the round end on your chest or back and listens. They can hear your heart going thump-thump and the air whooshing in your lungs. It does not hurt at all, though it can feel a bit cold.",
 jx:"The doctor let me listen to my own heartbeat through the stethoscope."},
{id:"m_hormone",cat:"med",t:"Hormone",
 s:"A chemical messenger made by a gland and carried in the blood to control processes in other parts of the body.",
 l:"Hormones regulate growth, sleep, hunger, mood, stress responses, blood sugar and development during puberty. Glands that produce them include the thyroid, the adrenal glands, the pancreas and the pituitary gland at the base of the brain. Because they travel through the blood, hormones can affect many organs at once, but each one acts only on cells with the right receptors. Too much or too little of a hormone can cause illness, and many such conditions can be treated.",
 x:"Insulin is a hormone that helps the body use sugar from food.",
 f:"The word hormone was introduced in 1905 by the British physiologist Ernest Starling, from a Greek word meaning to set in motion.",
 js:"A tiny chemical message that travels in your blood.",
 jl:"Your body sends messages in two ways: fast ones through nerves, and slower ones through the blood. The slower messengers are called hormones. They help you grow, tell you when you are hungry or sleepy, and get you ready to run when you are scared.",
 jx:"Growth hormone works mostly while you sleep, so a good night's rest helps you grow."},
{id:"m_ultrasound",cat:"med",t:"Ultrasound scan",
 s:"A medical test that uses high-pitched sound waves to create pictures of the inside of the body.",
 l:"A handheld probe sends out sound waves too high for people to hear and picks up their echoes as they bounce off organs and tissues. A computer turns the echoes into a moving image on a screen. Ultrasound is widely used in pregnancy to check on the baby, and also to examine the heart, the liver, the kidneys, blood vessels and muscles. It does not use X-rays and is generally painless, though cold gel is spread on the skin first.",
 x:"At the ultrasound scan we saw the baby's heart beating.",
 f:"The Glasgow doctor Ian Donald was one of the pioneers of using ultrasound in pregnancy, publishing key work in 1958.",
 js:"A test that uses sound to make a picture of the inside of your body.",
 jl:"For an ultrasound, a doctor puts some cold, slippery gel on your skin and moves a little wand over it. The wand sends out sounds that are too high to hear. Their echoes make a picture on a screen. It is a bit like how bats find their way in the dark.",
 jx:"Mum showed me the ultrasound picture of my baby sister before she was born."},
{id:"m_antiseptic",cat:"med",t:"Antiseptic and disinfectant",
 s:"An antiseptic kills or slows germs on skin or wounds; a disinfectant does the same job on objects and surfaces.",
 l:"Both reduce the number of microbes that could cause infection, but they are made for different uses. Antiseptics are gentle enough for living tissue and are used to clean cuts or a patient's skin before an operation. Disinfectants are stronger and are used on floors, worktops and medical equipment; they should not be put on skin. Neither replaces simple washing with soap and water, which removes most germs from hands. Always follow the instructions on the label.",
 x:"The nurse cleaned the graze with an antiseptic wipe.",
 f:"In 1867 the British surgeon Joseph Lister published his results using carbolic acid to keep wounds clean, which greatly reduced deaths after surgery.",
 js:"Liquids that kill germs: one for cuts, one for surfaces.",
 jl:"When you scrape your knee, a grown-up might clean it with an antiseptic, which is gentle on skin and kills germs. For cleaning tables or the bathroom, people use a disinfectant, which is much stronger and should never go on your skin.",
 jx:"The school nurse dabbed antiseptic on my knee, and it stung a little."},
{id:"m_en_999",cat:"med",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"999",
 s:"The emergency telephone number in the United Kingdom for ambulance, police, fire service and coastguard.",
 l:"Calling 999 connects you to an operator who asks which service you need, then passes you to that service's call handler. It is for emergencies where life is at risk or a crime is happening, such as someone who is unconscious, has severe chest pain or is badly hurt. For urgent but not life-threatening health problems, NHS 111 is the right number. The European emergency number 112 also works in the UK and is connected in the same way. Calls are free.",
 x:"He found his neighbour unconscious and called 999 straight away.",
 f:"The 999 service started in London on 30 June 1937, making it the oldest emergency telephone number in the world.",
 js:"The number to call in Britain in a real emergency.",
 jl:"If someone is badly hurt, there is a fire or you are in danger, you can phone 999. A calm person will answer and ask what is wrong. They will send an ambulance, a fire engine or the police. Only use it for real emergencies, never as a joke.",
 jx:"In our first-aid lesson we practised saying our address clearly, as if we were calling 999."},
/* ---------- LAW (+6) ---------- */
{id:"l_employment_contract",cat:"law",c:"Great Britain",t:"Employment contract",
 s:"The agreement between an employer and an employee setting out the terms of a job, such as pay, hours and duties.",
 l:"An employment contract exists as soon as a job offer is accepted, even if nothing is signed, but employees in Great Britain have a legal right to a written statement of the main terms from their first day of work. It should cover pay, working hours, holiday entitlement, notice periods and the job title. Some rights come from the law and cannot be removed by the contract, such as the National Minimum Wage and paid holiday. Changes to the contract usually need the employee's agreement.",
 x:"Before starting the new job, she read her employment contract carefully.",
 f:"Since April 2020, workers in Great Britain must receive their written statement of terms on or before their first day, not within two months as before.",
 js:"The agreement that says what a job is, how many hours and how much pay.",
 jl:"When grown-ups start a job, they agree with their boss what they will do, when they will work and how much they will be paid. That agreement is called an employment contract. It helps make sure everyone is treated fairly.",
 jx:"My aunt signed her employment contract on her first day at the bakery."},
{id:"l_patent",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Patent",
 s:"A legal right that lets an inventor stop others from making, using or selling their invention for a limited time.",
 l:"To get a patent, an invention must be new, involve an inventive step that is not obvious to an expert, and be capable of being made or used in industry. In the United Kingdom applications go to the Intellectual Property Office. A patent can last up to twenty years if renewal fees are paid each year. In exchange for this protection, the inventor must publish a detailed description, so others can learn from it and use it freely once the patent expires.",
 x:"The engineer applied for a patent on her new kind of folding wheelchair.",
 f:"The word comes from the Latin litterae patentes, meaning open letters: official royal documents that anyone could read.",
 js:"A special right that protects a new invention.",
 jl:"If you invent something brand new, like a clever new kind of bicycle bell, you can ask for a patent. It means that for some years nobody else may make or sell your invention without your permission. Later, everyone can use the idea.",
 jx:"My grandad got a patent for his invention many years ago."},
{id:"l_legal_aid",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Legal aid",
 s:"Help with the cost of legal advice or representation, paid for by the state for people who could not otherwise afford it.",
 l:"In England and Wales legal aid is run by the Legal Aid Agency. For civil matters, such as housing problems, some family cases or debt that threatens your home, it is usually available only if the case is of a certain type and your income and savings are low enough. In criminal cases, anyone questioned at a police station has the right to free advice from a duty solicitor, whatever their income. Organisations such as Citizens Advice can help people check whether they qualify.",
 x:"She qualified for legal aid, so she did not have to pay her solicitor herself.",
 f:"The modern legal aid system in England and Wales was set up by the Legal Aid and Advice Act of 1949.",
 js:"Free or cheaper help from a lawyer for people who cannot pay.",
 jl:"Lawyers can be expensive. So that everyone can get fair help, the government sometimes pays for a lawyer for people who do not have much money. This is called legal aid.",
 jx:"Mum explained that legal aid helps families who cannot afford a lawyer."},
{id:"l_age_criminal_responsibility",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",j:false,t:"Age of criminal responsibility",
 s:"The youngest age at which a person can be charged with and tried for a crime.",
 l:"In England and Wales the age of criminal responsibility is ten, which is low compared with many other European countries. Children aged ten to seventeen who are accused of crimes are usually dealt with by youth courts, which are less formal than adult courts and focus on preventing further offending. Children under ten cannot be charged, though other measures can be taken to protect them and others. Scotland sets the age differently, at twelve.",
 x:"The debate about raising the age of criminal responsibility has continued for years.",
 f:"Youth courts in England and Wales are usually closed to the public, and the identity of young defendants is normally protected."},
{id:"l_parental_responsibility",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Parental responsibility",
 s:"The legal rights and duties that a parent has towards a child, such as caring for them and making decisions about their upbringing.",
 l:"In England and Wales the term comes from the Children Act 1989. A person with parental responsibility can make important decisions about a child's schooling, medical treatment, religion and home. A mother automatically has it, and so does a father who was married to the mother or is named on the birth certificate for a birth registered since December 2003. Others, such as step-parents or grandparents, can gain it through an agreement or a court order. It lasts until the child turns eighteen.",
 x:"Both parents had parental responsibility, so the school needed to keep both informed.",
 f:"The Children Act 1989 says that when a court decides a question about a child's upbringing, the child's welfare must be its paramount consideration.",
 js:"The duty of grown-ups to look after their children and make good choices for them.",
 jl:"Parents and carers have an important job: to keep their children safe, healthy and learning. The law calls this parental responsibility. It means they make big decisions, like which school a child goes to, always trying to do what is best for the child.",
 jx:"My dad signed the form for the school trip because he has parental responsibility."},
{id:"l_en_cps",cat:"law",only:1,c:"England & Wales",t:"Crown Prosecution Service",
 s:"The independent public body that decides whether people in England and Wales should be charged with a crime and then presents the case in court.",
 l:"The police investigate crimes and gather evidence, but in most serious cases it is a prosecutor from the Crown Prosecution Service, usually called the CPS, who decides whether to charge someone. They apply two tests: whether there is enough evidence for a realistic prospect of conviction, and whether prosecution is in the public interest. CPS lawyers then prepare the case and present it in court. The service is led by the Director of Public Prosecutions.",
 x:"The Crown Prosecution Service decided there was not enough evidence to bring the case to court.",
 f:"The CPS was created by the Prosecution of Offences Act 1985 and began work in 1986; before that, the police usually prosecuted cases themselves.",
 js:"The lawyers in England who decide if a case should go to court.",
 jl:"When the police think someone has broken the law, they collect clues. Then special lawyers check the clues and decide whether there is enough proof to go to court. In England and Wales these lawyers work for the Crown Prosecution Service.",
 jx:"On the news they said the Crown Prosecution Service would look at the evidence."},
/* ---------- EVERYDAY LANGUAGE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"g_anagram",cat:"gen",t:"Anagram",
 s:"A word or phrase made by rearranging all the letters of another word or phrase.",
 l:"To make an anagram, you use every letter of the original exactly once in a new order. Listen becomes silent, and earth becomes heart. Anagrams are a favourite of crossword setters, who hide them in clues, and of word-game fans. Writers have sometimes used anagrams of their own names as pen names. A good anagram feels surprising because the new word seems to comment on the old one.",
 x:"The crossword clue was an anagram of the word Elvis, which gave lives.",
 f:"The French writer François Rabelais published some of his work under the name Alcofribas Nasier, an anagram of his real name.",
 js:"A word made by mixing up the letters of another word.",
 jl:"Take the word listen and shuffle its letters. You can make silent. That is an anagram. Try the word act: if you move the letters around, you get cat. Anagrams are like puzzles hidden inside words.",
 jx:"My sister found an anagram of her name and wrote it on her door."},
{id:"g_portmanteau",cat:"gen",t:"Portmanteau word",
 s:"A word formed by blending parts of two other words and combining their meanings.",
 l:"Unlike a simple compound, a portmanteau word squeezes pieces of two words together. Breakfast plus lunch gives brunch, smoke plus fog gives smog, and motor plus hotel gives motel. English keeps creating new blends, especially in technology and the media, such as podcast, from iPod and broadcast. Many begin as jokes or advertising slogans and some become everyday words.",
 x:"Brunch is a portmanteau word, and so is the late morning meal it describes.",
 f:"The term was popularised by Lewis Carroll in Through the Looking-Glass, where Humpty Dumpty explains that slithy means lithe and slimy, like a portmanteau, an old type of suitcase with two parts.",
 js:"A word made by squashing two words together.",
 jl:"Some words are made by mixing two words into one, like a smoothie of words. Breakfast and lunch become brunch. Smoke and fog become smog. You can try inventing your own, like a spork, which is a spoon and a fork at once.",
 jx:"We ate with a spork at the picnic, and I learned it is a portmanteau word."},
{id:"g_eponym",cat:"gen",t:"Eponym",
 s:"A word that comes from the name of a person, or the person whose name gave rise to it.",
 l:"Many everyday words began as names. The sandwich is linked to John Montagu, the Fourth Earl of Sandwich, the cardigan to the Earl of Cardigan, and the saxophone to its Belgian inventor Adolphe Sax. Units in science are often eponyms too, like the watt and the newton. Over time, people may forget the person and treat the word as ordinary, so it is often written with a small letter.",
 x:"The saxophone is an eponym, named after the instrument maker Adolphe Sax.",
 f:"The verb to boycott comes from Charles Boycott, a land agent in Ireland whom local people refused to deal with in 1880.",
 js:"A word that comes from someone's name.",
 jl:"Some words are named after real people. The sandwich got its name from a man called the Earl of Sandwich. The saxophone is named after Mr Sax, who invented it. These words are called eponyms.",
 jx:"I didn't know the word sandwich came from a person's name."},
{id:"g_proverb",cat:"gen",t:"Proverb",
 s:"A short, well-known saying that expresses a piece of common wisdom or advice.",
 l:"Proverbs pass on experience in a memorable form, often using rhyme, rhythm or a vivid picture. The early bird catches the worm encourages starting early, while don't count your chickens before they hatch warns against expecting success too soon. Many proverbs exist in similar forms in many languages, though the images change. Some contradict each other, such as many hands make light work and too many cooks spoil the broth, which shows they are rules of thumb rather than laws.",
 x:"Grandma quoted the proverb that practice makes perfect when I complained about piano lessons.",
 f:"The study of proverbs is called paremiology, from the Greek word paroimía, meaning a saying.",
 js:"A short wise saying that people have used for a long time.",
 jl:"A proverb is a little saying full of advice. Practice makes perfect means you get better by trying again and again. Look before you leap means think before you do something. Grandparents often know lots of them.",
 jx:"My teacher said the proverb better late than never when I finally handed in my homework."},
{id:"g_slang",cat:"gen",t:"Slang",
 s:"Very informal words and expressions used mainly in speech, often by a particular group, such as young people.",
 l:"Slang changes quickly: words that sound fresh to one generation can seem old-fashioned to the next. It helps a group feel connected and can show who belongs, which is why teenagers, sports fans and online communities develop their own. Some slang eventually becomes standard language, while most disappears. It is fine in relaxed conversation but usually avoided in formal writing, such as job applications or essays.",
 x:"My parents don't understand half the slang my little brother picks up at school.",
 f:"The origin of the word slang itself is unknown; it first appears in writing in the eighteenth century.",
 js:"Very casual words that friends use when chatting.",
 jl:"Slang is the relaxed, fun way of speaking that friends use with each other. Each group of friends, and each generation, has its own favourite slang words. You use them when chatting, but not in a story for school or a letter to the head teacher.",
 jx:"Grandpa laughed when I used a new slang word he had never heard."},
{id:"g_en_fortnight",cat:"gen",only:1,t:"Fortnight",
 s:"A period of two weeks; a word common in British English but rarely used in American English.",
 l:"People in Britain often say they are going on holiday for a fortnight or that something happens every fortnight, meaning every two weeks. In the United States people would normally just say two weeks, and many Americans find the word old-fashioned. It is also used in Australia, Ireland and other Commonwealth countries. The related word fortnightly describes something that happens once every two weeks, such as a magazine or a bin collection.",
 x:"The recycling bins are collected once a fortnight on our street.",
 f:"Fortnight comes from the Old English phrase for fourteen nights, because people once counted periods of time in nights rather than days.",
 js:"Two weeks.",
 jl:"In Britain, people often say a fortnight when they mean two weeks. If your summer holiday lasts a fortnight, you are away for fourteen days. The word comes from fourteen nights.",
 jx:"Our swimming lessons are every fortnight, so I go twice a month."},
/* ---------- PSYCHOLOGY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"p_boundaries",cat:"psy",t:"Personal boundaries",
 s:"The limits a person sets about what they are comfortable with in how others treat them, physically and emotionally.",
 l:"Boundaries can be about touch, personal space, time, privacy, belongings or the way people speak to us. Healthy boundaries help relationships work because everyone knows what is okay. Setting one can be as simple as saying, I need a break now, or, please knock before coming in. People's boundaries differ, and they can change depending on the situation. Respecting other people's limits matters as much as stating our own, and saying no is allowed.",
 x:"Telling her colleagues she would not answer emails at weekends was an important boundary.",
 f:"The anthropologist Edward T. Hall studied how much personal space people keep from each other and described it in his 1966 book The Hidden Dimension.",
 js:"Your own rules about what feels okay and what does not.",
 jl:"Everyone has invisible lines about what feels comfortable. Maybe you do not like being tickled, or you need quiet time after school. These are your boundaries. It is okay to say stop or no, thank you, and it is kind to listen when others say it too.",
 jx:"I told my cousin I don't like hugs from behind, and he respected my boundary."},
{id:"p_emotion_regulation",cat:"psy",t:"Emotion regulation",
 s:"The ways people notice, understand and manage their feelings so they can respond in a way that suits the situation.",
 l:"Regulating emotions does not mean hiding or ignoring them. It means having strategies to deal with strong feelings, such as pausing, breathing slowly, naming the feeling, moving the body, talking to someone or changing what we focus on. These skills develop through childhood and can be learned at any age. Some people, including many autistic people or people with ADHD, may experience feelings more intensely and benefit from strategies that suit them. Co-regulation, calming down with the help of a trusted person, is often the first step.",
 x:"Going for a short walk is one of her best tools for emotion regulation when she feels frustrated.",
 f:"Research led by Matthew Lieberman at the University of California, Los Angeles, published in 2007, found that putting feelings into words can reduce activity in a brain area linked with strong emotions.",
 js:"Ways to handle big feelings so they don't take over.",
 jl:"Sometimes feelings like anger or worry get really big, like a wave. Emotion regulation means finding ways to ride the wave. You might take slow breaths, squeeze a cushion, draw, or tell a grown-up how you feel. Every feeling is okay; we just learn what helps.",
 jx:"When I got upset, I used my calm-down corner, which helps with emotion regulation."},
{id:"p_maslow",cat:"psy",t:"Maslow's hierarchy of needs",
 s:"A theory by the psychologist Abraham Maslow that arranges human needs in levels, from basic survival to personal growth.",
 l:"Maslow suggested that people are motivated first by physical needs such as food, water and sleep, then by safety, then by love and belonging, then by esteem, and finally by self-actualisation, which means becoming the fullest version of yourself. The idea is often shown as a pyramid. It is widely taught in schools, nursing and business, but researchers point out that people do not always meet needs in a strict order, and that needs differ across cultures.",
 x:"The teacher used Maslow's hierarchy of needs to explain why hungry pupils struggle to concentrate.",
 f:"Maslow described the theory in a paper in 1943, but the famous pyramid drawing was created later by other writers, not by Maslow himself.",
 js:"An idea that people need food and safety first, then love and then chances to grow.",
 jl:"A scientist named Abraham Maslow had an idea about what people need. First we need food, water, sleep and a safe home. Then we need friends and family who love us. When those needs are met, we can grow, learn and do our best.",
 jx:"Our class drew Maslow's pyramid and wrote food and sleep on the bottom step."},
{id:"p_framing_effect",cat:"psy",t:"Framing effect",
 s:"The tendency for people to make different choices depending on how the same information is presented.",
 l:"An exam described as having an eighty percent pass rate sounds easier than one with a twenty percent failure rate, although the two are the same. Likewise, a treatment with a ninety percent survival rate seems more appealing than one with a ten percent death rate. Framing works because we react to gains and losses emotionally, not only to the numbers. Advertisers, politicians and health campaigns all use it, so it helps to restate information in the opposite way before deciding.",
 x:"The framing effect made the deal sound better: save twenty percent instead of pay eighty percent.",
 f:"The psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman described the effect in a famous 1981 paper; Kahneman later received the Nobel Prize in economics in 2002.",
 js:"When the way something is said changes what we choose.",
 jl:"Imagine one sign says half full and another says half empty, about the same glass. They mean exactly the same, but they feel different. How we describe something can change how people feel about it. That is the framing effect.",
 jx:"The shop said only two biscuits left to make us hurry, which is a bit of a framing effect."},
{id:"p_active_listening",cat:"psy",t:"Active listening",
 s:"Listening with full attention, trying to understand the other person and showing them that you have understood.",
 l:"Active listening means more than staying quiet while someone talks. The listener focuses on the speaker, avoids planning a reply or interrupting, asks open questions and sums up what they heard in their own words, for example, so you felt left out when that happened. It also means noticing feelings, not only facts. Counsellors, teachers, doctors and mediators use it, and it helps in friendships and families. People who find eye contact uncomfortable can listen actively in other ways.",
 x:"Active listening helped the manager understand what was really worrying her team.",
 f:"The term active listening was introduced by the psychologists Carl Rogers and Richard Farson in a paper in 1957.",
 js:"Listening carefully and showing you really understand.",
 jl:"Active listening means listening with your whole attention. You let your friend finish, you do not jump in, and then you say back what you heard, like, so you were sad because the game ended. It helps people feel heard and understood.",
 jx:"When my friend told me about her lost cat, I practised active listening."},
{id:"p_delayed_gratification",cat:"psy",t:"Delayed gratification",
 s:"Choosing to wait for a bigger or better reward later instead of taking a smaller one straight away.",
 l:"Saving pocket money for something special, or finishing homework before playing, are everyday examples. The ability to wait depends on many things: a person's age, their tiredness or stress, and whether they trust that the later reward will really come. Strategies such as distracting yourself, hiding the temptation or making a plan make waiting easier. It is a skill that grows, not a fixed trait, and for people with ADHD the environment and support can make a big difference.",
 x:"Saving for the bike for three months was a real exercise in delayed gratification.",
 f:"In the famous marshmallow test at Stanford University around 1970, Walter Mischel let children choose one treat now or two later; a larger study in 2018 found the link with later success was much weaker than early reports suggested.",
 js:"Waiting for something better instead of having something smaller right now.",
 jl:"Imagine you can have one sticker now or three stickers tomorrow. Waiting for the three is called delayed gratification. Waiting can be hard. It helps to put the treat out of sight, play a game or think about something else.",
 jx:"I saved my pocket money for weeks instead of buying sweets, and got a new book."}
]);
